WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.640
<v Christina>Percolate pushes everything into Postgres. The agent

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.600
<v Christina>registry, the tool definitions, the model routing, the

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.140
<v Christina>session state, the loop itself. Why go that far?

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.120
<v Chris>Because compressing it that far shows you what is

5
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.460
<v Chris>actually in there. Most of an agentic system is data,

6
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.840
<v Chris>even though the frameworks around it are all written

7
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.840
<v Chris>in Python, and pushing it down is how you see that.

8
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.840
<v Christina>So what survives the compression?

9
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.520
<v Chris>One real engine that calls a model, parses what comes

10
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.500
<v Chris>back, works out whether that was a tool call, and goes

11
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.980
<v Chris>round again. That part is genuine software and it's

12
00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.900
<v Chris>fiddly. Almost everything around it is a schema.

13
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.100
<v Christina>And the part that stays hard?

14
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.540
<v Chris>Orchestration. What is supposed to happen, what

15
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.300
<v Chris>already happened, what to do when a step fails at

16
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.900
<v Chris>three in the morning, and who is allowed to see the

17
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.280
<v Chris>result. That's a database problem wearing an AI hat.

18
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.860
<v Christina>Give me the concrete version. What's a row that I'd

19
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.680
<v Christina>expect to be code?

20
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.100
<v Chris>A prompt is data. A tool definition is an OpenAPI

21
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.440
<v Chris>document or an MCP manifest in a JSON column. An agent

22
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.380
<v Chris>is a row, a skill is a row, the model registry is

23
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.800
<v Chris>rows. A conversation is turns with a foreign key to a

24
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:12.800
<v Chris>session.

25
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.760
<v Christina>So adding an agent is what, exactly?

26
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.140
<v Chris>An insert. The capability is authored rather than

27
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.540
<v Chris>deployed, which means what an agent may do is

28
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.840
<v Chris>something you can query and scope and audit, instead

29
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.400
<v Chris>of a fact about whichever process happens to be

30
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:28.720
<v Chris>running.

31
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.880
<v Christina>In most stacks, permissions and workflow are what you

32
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.240
<v Christina>bolt on at the end, once the interesting part works.

33
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.500
<v Chris>Those are the two we built first. Permissions sit

34
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.560
<v Chris>underneath everything rather than in front of it:

35
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.700
<v Chris>users, roles, API keys, sessions and row-level

36
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.460
<v Chris>security.

37
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.840
<v Christina>Why underneath?

38
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.720
<v Chris>The moment an agent can query your data, which rows a

39
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.960
<v Chris>caller may see is the same question as what that agent

40
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.840
<v Chris>is allowed to know. If that lives above the database

41
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.240
<v Chris>there is always one more path to the data that forgot

42
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:00.840
<v Chris>to ask.

43
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.940
<v Christina>So where is the decision taken?

44
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.380
<v Chris>Where the data is. The tools an agent may call, the

45
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.919
<v Chris>rows a query returns, the files a run may read and the

46
00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:14.320
<v Chris>tasks a worker may claim are all one authorization

47
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.700
<v Chris>model, not one invented per service.

48
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.400
<v Christina>And the workflow engine got the same promotion.

49
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.720
<v Chris>It went underneath the agent runtime rather than

50
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:23.380
<v Chris>beside it.

51
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.180
<v Christina>Say I bring you a domain of my own. Shipping, claims

52
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.960
<v Christina>processing, whatever it is.

53
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.900
<v Chris>The work splits four ways and it's the same four every

54
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.060
<v Chris>time. Where the data comes from, what kinds of thing

55
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.460
<v Chris>exist in your world, the agents and tools that do the

56
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.700
<v Chris>judging, and then the work itself.

57
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.700
<v Christina>And a package bundles all four?

58
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.060
<v Chris>A plugin records which bundle each thing came from, so

59
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.180
<v Chris>servers and skills and agents install and uninstall

60
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.000
<v Chris>together. A package widens that same document to carry

61
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.360
<v Chris>your data sources and your vocabulary as well, and the

62
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.420
<v Chris>whole thing applies in one transaction.

63
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:00.380
<v Christina>Including the sources.

64
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.140
<v Chris>They come with it, and that's what makes it a domain

65
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.280
<v Chris>rather than a toolbox. Every row it creates is stamped

66
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.320
<v Chris>with the package that created it, so uninstalling

67
00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.120
<v Chris>prunes exactly what was put there.

68
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.240
<v Christina>Let's stay on sources, because that's usually where

69
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.900
<v Christina>these systems get thin.

70
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.540
<v Chris>Sourcing is one of the five subsystems here, not an

71
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.680
<v Chris>import script somebody wrote once. A channel is a row

72
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.920
<v Chris>saying where data comes from and how to reach it, and

73
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.920
<v Chris>uploaded content is parsed, chunked and embedded on

74
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:29.540
<v Chris>the way in.

75
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.680
<v Christina>I'm still not moving my warehouse into your database.

76
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.680
<v Chris>You don't have to. What Percolate keeps is how to

77
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.920
<v Chris>reach the service: the connection, the credential, the

78
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.880
<v Chris>schedule. Pulling from it is a workflow you author and

79
00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.760
<v Chris>schedule like any other, so the pipeline feeding the

80
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.400
<v Chris>agent and the agent itself are the same machinery.

81
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.980
<v Christina>Which is the part that gets underestimated.

82
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.140
<v Chris>The hard part of an AI product is the dozen

83
00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.460
<v Chris>unglamorous steps that have to run every night so the

84
00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.060
<v Chris>clever bit has something to work with.

85
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.300
<v Christina>Once it's all in there, how do I ask across it? Some

86
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.880
<v Christina>of it is graph-shaped and some is just text.

87
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.580
<v Chris>There's one query surface over all of it, and the

88
00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.760
<v Chris>modes are named after the moves an investigation is

89
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.920
<v Chris>made of. Resolve a name to a thing. Walk out from it

90
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.220
<v Chris>and see what it touches. Find things that mean roughly

91
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:18.779
<v Chris>the same.

92
00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.940
<v Christina>Why put a layer above SQL, though? People know SQL.

93
00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.540
<v Chris>Because of the way the mistakes fail. Compare a vector

94
00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.140
<v Chris>built by one model against a space built by another

95
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.380
<v Chris>and you don't get an error. You get a number, and it

96
00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:33.820
<v Chris>looks like every other number.

97
00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.080
<v Christina>And a model writing that query never gets to look at

98
00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.320
<v Christina>the result and frown.

99
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.520
<v Chris>It doesn't have the option, so we refuse that one when

100
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.920
<v Chris>it compiles, before anything runs.

101
00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.880
<v Christina>I want to push on workflow semantics, because plenty

102
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.300
<v Christina>of systems say the word and mean something thin by it.

103
00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.060
<v Chris>What we mean is a run being a graph of steps with real

104
00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.620
<v Chris>dependencies, retries with backoff, compensation when

105
00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.500
<v Chris>something has to be undone, and state that survives

106
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.760
<v Chris>the process dying halfway.

107
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.380
<v Christina>That's a data pipeline. There's nothing especially AI

108
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.040
<v Christina>about it.

109
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.900
<v Chris>An agent framework and a workflow engine are the same

110
00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.380
<v Chris>shape. A step is one of six kinds, and one of them

111
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.340
<v Chris>happens to call a model. Two of them execute inside

112
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.320
<v Chris>Postgres with no process running anywhere.

113
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.240
<v Christina>So retries on an agent step come from the same place

114
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.560
<v Christina>as on a SQL step.

115
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.980
<v Chris>Written once, applying to every kind, because an agent

116
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.520
<v Chris>step isn't a special case.

117
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.300
<v Christina>Here's the one I think gets handled badly. A step

118
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.660
<v Christina>needs a person to approve something, and she's asleep,

119
00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.880
<v Christina>and she'll get to it in six hours.

120
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.120
<v Chris>A scheduler has three options there and none of them

121
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:45.960
<v Chris>are good. It can hold the slot, so something sits

122
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.280
<v Chris>doing nothing for six hours. It can time out and lose

123
00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.340
<v Chris>the work. Or it can escalate, which just interrupts

124
00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:53.920
<v Chris>somebody else.

125
00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.020
<v Christina>So what happens instead?

126
00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.580
<v Chris>Nothing at all. The task moves into a waiting state

127
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.760
<v Chris>and stops costing anything, because it's a row. When

128
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.480
<v Chris>she answers, that resolves it and the run carries on

129
00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.340
<v Chris>from where it stopped.

130
00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.440
<v Christina>And she can't approve something she was never meant to

131
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:11.700
<v Christina>see.

132
00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.780
<v Chris>That's the same check as reading the row would be,

133
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.040
<v Chris>which is what having one authorization model

134
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.500
<v Chris>underneath everything buys you.

135
00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:22.260
<v Christina>This runs across a cluster somewhere. What coordinates

136
00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:22.680
<v Christina>it?

137
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.340
<v Chris>Nothing does. Postgres is the queue. Workers claim

138
00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.000
<v Chris>work off it rather than having it handed to them, and

139
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.080
<v Chris>no single component is holding the plan.

140
00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.860
<v Christina>What if I lose a worker halfway through a run?

141
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.760
<v Chris>Throughput drops and everything keeps going. There's

142
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.360
<v Chris>nothing to fail over, because there was never a

143
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.200
<v Chris>coordinator to fail.

144
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.800
<v Christina>So the pitch isn't really a better agent.

145
00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.460
<v Chris>It's one control plane. Your sources, your vocabulary,

146
00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.440
<v Chris>your agents, your workflows and your permissions, all

147
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.780
<v Chris>in one place, as data you can query.

148
00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.600
<v Christina>Which is where the work was the whole time.

149
00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.200
<v Chris>It's the part we'd build first if we started again.
